rules.details:
Use macros to declare reflectable fields while storing type-safe values through templates.

C++ Example:
```cpp
#include <variant>
#include <string>

template<typename T>
struct ReflectField {
    std::string name;
    T* ptr;
    template<typename U>
    U get() { return *ptr; }  // Type-safe access
};

#define reflect_int(name) int name; static ReflectField<int> __rf_##name{#name, &ThisClass::name};
#define reflect_double(name) double name; static ReflectField<double> __rf_##name{#name, &ThisClass::name};
#define reflect_string(name) std::string name; static ReflectField<std::string> __rf_##name{#name, &ThisClass::name};
#define reflect_bool(name) bool name; static ReflectField<bool> __rf_##name{#name, &ThisClass::name};
```
Existing string-based access can be preserved through `std::to_string` or custom conversions.

Record member method information through macros and templates, and provide a type-safe invocation interface.

C++ Example:
```cpp
template<typename Ret, typename... Args>
struct ReflectMethod {
    std::string name;
    Ret(ThisClass::*method)(Args...);
    Ret invoke(ThisClass* obj, Args... args) { return (obj->*method)(args...); }
};

#define reflect_method_0(name, ret) static ReflectMethod<ret> __rm_##name{#name, &ThisClass::name};
#define reflect_method_1(name, ret, A1) static ReflectMethod<ret, A1> __rm_##name{#name, &ThisClass::name};
// Can be extended to N arguments
```
- To support method overloading, macros can generate registration variables with different names.
- `invoke` provides type-safe calls; the number and types of arguments must match the macro.

Generate factory functions through macros and templates to enable dynamic object creation.

C++ Example:
```cpp
template<typename T, typename... Args>
struct ReflectConstructor {
    T* (*factory)(Args...);
    T* create(Args... args) { return factory(args...); }
};

#define reflect_constructor_0() static ReflectConstructor<ThisClass> __rc_{[](){ return new ThisClass(); }};
#define reflect_constructor_1(A1) static ReflectConstructor<ThisClass, A1> __rc_{[](A1 a1){ return new ThisClass(a1); }};
```
- The factory function will be registered in the base class Object's class registry for dynamic lookup by class name.

Each class stores a pointer to its parent's ReflectClass metadata. When querying for a field or method, if it's not found in the current class, the search will recursively proceed up the inheritance chain.

C++ Example:
```cpp
struct ReflectClass {
    std::string name;
    ReflectClass* parent;
    std::vector<void*> fields; // Can store ReflectField/T* pointers
    std::vector<void*> methods;
};
```
- Supports single and multi-level inheritance. Users need to avoid issues caused by multiple or circular inheritance.
